VALUES (
  'acfe2e12-0c08-54d2-8fb0-5bd8c21517ef',
  '81754945-cdc5-5d4d-845b-c28d2259eb1d',
  '8cf19bb005c2ab3e',
  'How to make meetings better',
  'THINK
The user wants me to translate an English lesson title "How to make meetings better" into Vietnamese.
I need to follow these requirements:
- **Accurate and natural:** The meaning should be preserved, and it should sound like something a Vietnamese speaker would say.
- **Concise:** It''s a title, so no unnecessary words.
- **Return only the translated title:** No extra text, no explanations.
- **Proper Vietnamese grammar and natural expressions, correct tone marks.**

Let''s break down the English title:
- "How to": This implies a guide, a method, or instructions. Common Vietnamese equivalents are "CÃ¡ch...", "LÃ m tháº¿ nÃ o Ä‘á»ƒ...", "BÃ­ quyáº¿t...".
- "make meetings better": This means improving meetings, making them more effective, productive, or enjoyable.

Possible Vietnamese translations for "make meetings better":
- "cáº£i thiá»‡n cuá»™c há»p" (improve meetings) - sounds good, quite direct.
- "nÃ¢ng cao cháº¥t lÆ°á»£ng cuá»™c há»p" (enhance meeting quality) - a bit longer, but also good.
- "tá»‘i Æ°u hÃ³a cuá»™c há»p" (optimize meetings) - a bit more formal/business-like.
- "Ä‘á»ƒ cuá»™c há»p hiá»‡u quáº£ hÆ¡n" (to make meetings more effective) - good, but "Ä‘á»ƒ" might be redundant with "CÃ¡ch".

Combining "How to" with "make meetings better":

1.  **CÃ¡ch cáº£i thiá»‡n cuá»™c há»p:**
    *   "CÃ¡ch" (How to/Method to)
    *   "cáº£i thiá»‡n" (improve)
    *   "cuá»™c há»p" (meeting/meetings)
    *   This is concise, accurate, and natural.

2.  **LÃ m tháº¿ nÃ o Ä‘á»ƒ cuá»™c há»p tá»‘t hÆ¡n:**
    *   "LÃ m tháº¿ nÃ o Ä‘á»ƒ" (How to)
    *   "cuá»™c há»p" (meeting/meetings)
    *   "tá»‘t hÆ¡n" (better)
    *   This is also good, but "CÃ¡ch cáº£i thiá»‡n" feels slightly more direct and title-like. "Tá»‘t hÆ¡n" is a direct translation of "better", but "cáº£i thiá»‡n" (improve) often sounds more professional in this context than just "tá»‘t hÆ¡n".

3.  **BÃ­ quyáº¿t há»p hiá»‡u quáº£:**
    *   "BÃ­ quyáº¿t" (Secret/Tip for)
    *   "há»p" (to meet/meeting - used as a noun here)
    *   "hiá»‡u quáº£" (effective)
    *   This is very natural and common for titles. "Hiá»‡u quáº£" (effective) implies "better" in the context of meetings.

Let''s compare "CÃ¡ch cáº£i thiá»‡n cuá»™c há»p" and "BÃ­ quyáº¿t há»p hiá»‡u quáº£".
- "CÃ¡ch cáº£i thiá»‡n cuá»™c há»p" is a very direct translation of "How to improve meetings". It''s clear and concise.
- "BÃ­ quyáº¿t há»p hiá»‡u quáº£" implies "Tips for effective meetings" or "Secrets to effective meetings". "Hiá»‡u quáº£" (effective) is a strong synonym for "better" in this context. It''s also very natural for a title.

The English "make meetings better" is quite broad. "Better" can mean more effective, more productive, shorter, more engaging, etc.
"Cáº£i thiá»‡n" (improve) covers this well.
"Hiá»‡u quáº£" (effective) also covers this well, often being the primary goal of making meetings "better".

Considering conciseness and naturalness for a *lesson title*:
"CÃ¡ch cáº£i thiá»‡n cuá»™c há»p" is excellent.
"BÃ­ quyáº¿t há»p hiá»‡u quáº£" is also excellent, perhaps even slightly more engaging as a title.

Let''s re-evaluate "How to make meetings better". It''s about the *process* or *method* of making them better.
"CÃ¡ch" (How to/Method) fits perfectly.
"Cáº£i thiá»‡n" (improve) directly addresses "make better".
"Cuá»™c há»p" (meetings).

So, "CÃ¡ch cáº£i thiá»‡n cuá»™c há»p" seems to be the most direct, accurate, concise, and natural translation that covers the broadness of "better".

Let''s consider the nuance of "better".
- "Tá»‘t hÆ¡n" (better) is direct. "CÃ¡ch lÃ m cuá»™c há»p tá»‘t hÆ¡n" (How to make meetings better) is grammatically correct but a bit wordy for a title.
- "Cáº£i thiá»‡n" (improve) implies making something better. "CÃ¡ch cáº£i thiá»‡n cuá»™c há»p" is very strong.
- "Hiá»‡u quáº£ hÆ¡n" (more effective) is a specific kind of "better". "CÃ¡ch há»p hiá»‡u quáº£ hÆ¡n" (How to meet more effectively) or "CÃ¡ch lÃ m cuá»™c há»p hiá»‡u quáº£ hÆ¡n" (How to make meetings more effective). The latter is too long. "CÃ¡ch há»p hiá»‡u quáº£ hÆ¡n" is good, but "cuá»™c há»p" (the meeting itself) is usually the subject of improvement, not just the act of "há»p" (meeting).

"CÃ¡ch cáº£i thiá»‡n cuá»™c há»p" is concise, uses "cáº£i thiá»‡n" which is a common and appropriate verb for improving processes/events like meetings, and "cuá»™c há»p" is the standard term for a meeting.

Final check:
- Accurate and natural: Yes.
- Concise: Yes.
- Only translated title: Yes.
- Proper grammar and tone marks: Yes.

I will go with "CÃ¡ch cáº£i thiá»‡n cuá»™c há»p".',
